{"questions":[{"question_id":"fiVb9ZmMM2eIsnsx","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2006","year":2006,"examDate":2006,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"periodic-table-and-periodicity","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"ionization-energy","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"the-increasing-order-of-the-first-ionization-enthalpies-of-t-2006-marks-4-fivb9zmmm2eisnsx.htm","content":"The increasing order of the first ionization enthalpies of the elements B, P, S and F (lowest first) is :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"F &lt; S &lt; P &lt; B "},{"identifier":"B","content":"P &lt; S &lt; B &lt; F"},{"identifier":"C","content":"B &lt; P &lt; S &lt; F "},{"identifier":"D","content":"B &lt; S &lt; P &lt; F "}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"The correct order of ionisation enthalpies is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.09ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.621ex\" height=\"1.686ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 6904.7 745\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D439\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D439\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1026.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2082.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3111.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4167.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5089.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6145.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>\n<br><br><b>NOTE :</b> On moving along a period ionization enthalpy increases from left to right and decreases from top to bottom in a group. But this trend breaks up in case of atom having fully or half filled stable orbitals. \n<br><br>In this case <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.699ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 751 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> has a stable half filled electronic configuration hence its ionisation enthalpy is greater in comparison to <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.088ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 923 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(645,0)\"><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> For B(5) electronic configuration is = 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sup>1</sup> and P(15) electronic configuration is = 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sup>6</sup>3s<sup>2</sup>3p<sup>3</sup>. As P has half filled valence shell so removing electron will be harder compare to B.  Hence by checking all options the correct possible order is\n<br><br>B &lt; S &lt; P &lt; F"}]}